 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049536 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049968 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049536 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129275 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049536 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129276 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049536 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129277 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049536 #edb/ref 1031 "The Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049536 #edb/ref 1033 2058 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049536 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "fc884f59-36fd-5f2b-a335-0ef7a9cd90cb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049645 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050396 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049645 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198403 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049645 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198404 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049645 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198405 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049645 #edb/ref 1031 "Computing Triple Integrals Using a Change of Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049645 #edb/ref 1033 4151 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049645 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "077676ad-6f2b-5e36-b6e9-f37bfd844a79" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048865 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048887 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050946 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198417 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198418 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198419 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198420 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1031 "The Derivative of a Multivariable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1033 4169 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186049968 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "220386bf-4147-5f25-bbd8-b3b4f855f1a0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048181 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049536 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050051 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050822 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124603 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124657 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124658 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1031 "Identifying Quadric Surfaces" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1033 1898 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050011 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "25b1fb2e-aa54-5955-88cd-263118f65faa" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128852 #edb/ref 1069 "e-11517" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128852 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n![](images/f8/f82cf760dee34626c290e29420fbaddb4ef254180087d05bb6fb883f2dc32e75.svg)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b22d04e0-8c6a-507a-9d76-ab05adb32b10" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128852 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation \n\n![](images/1c/1c2567cd1c2cf28ed17b6dc445025cb60061dc7d7d6c78c960be77f8866ef4a7.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad \nv(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad\nw(x,y,z) = yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x|\\begin{aligned}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x(xy-0) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y & =2\\cdot x\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot v \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128853 #edb/ref 1069 "e-11518" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128853 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n![](images/22/22690753019e406703952ae0c2a79450764b5ff42c758a03b2875a9d428d9e78.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b16845a2-5f66-524a-90ea-37f8a8186f8f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128853 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation \n\n![](images/be/be4c52a967cf6430f1d365ebb9b3a3a20bc852ac6422ed51e01dc5b39055c816.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w) =w-u^3, \\qquad \ny(u,v,w) = 2uv, \\qquad\nz(u,v,w) = u^2+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3{u}^{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 2u & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2v & 2u \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}(0-0)+1(2v-4{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} =0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2(v-2{u}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-2u^2 = 0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $v=2u^2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128854 #edb/ref 1069 "e-11579" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128854 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the Cartesian $uvw$-space onto the Cartesian $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx = 2u-v,\\qquad\ny = u+3v+w,\\qquad\nz = u+2v-w.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66d757d2-f2ad-5f0d-b4f1-8d426f4c8351" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186128854 #edb/ref 1074 "For a transformation \n\n![](images/6e/6e94e780387ccbba3d8b6a6aaf5f00fbf362cd8163837a8ea78ad31c18f3b1b3.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-3-2)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-10-2 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048257 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129734 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129757 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281335 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281336 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281337 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281338 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281340 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281341 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281342 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281343 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128852 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129275 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050011 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129532 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129760 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281344 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281345 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281346 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281347 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281348 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376130 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401281 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401288 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406802 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128853 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129276 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048225 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048511 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049165 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129733 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129756 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281331 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281332 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281333 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281334 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406803 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128854 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129277 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130216 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130217 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130218 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130219 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130235 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130240 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1045 "The Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130218 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049536 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186129394 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "caa59eff-71b4-5d09-9d46-37886ba2c5a7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130216 #edb/ref 1061 42965 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130216 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3cc8dd7f-188a-5699-96ad-6f7b673a3512" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130216 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129275 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130217 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130216 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130217 #edb/ref 1061 42966 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130217 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c37bbbe-760d-5104-b2e3-292b83880dd0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130217 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129276 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130218 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130240 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130218 #edb/ref 1061 43046 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130218 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8ced6e6-4bb6-52e9-8819-7a54180e8a4f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130218 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130219 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130235 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130219 #edb/ref 1061 43048 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130219 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3c03e1b-973d-566a-96d2-32f746decf45" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130219 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129277 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130235 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130217 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130235 #edb/ref 1061 44587 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99520515-6bfa-5265-8e42-e193dc1183f6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130469 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130240 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130219 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130240 #edb/ref 1061 44764 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ed9fa5db-a7c0-512a-bb14-c0e7e5dcbcd0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130473 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130463 #edb/ref 1064 "Similar to transformations in the plane, we can also consider transformations of three-dimensional regions.\n\nFor example, the transformation\n\n![](images/71/71b4c6b8f2db4890436050206115b94eb57b088be76049cb32667f02986b4f56.svg) \n\nmaps a rectangular prism in Cartesian $uvw$-space onto one-eighth of a sphere in Cartesian $xyz$-space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e1ee5a6116d57cc1c3419b55bf0a9701b9f5d4d93931d053ed4a05b83a5d8f0.png)\n\n \n\nIn general, if a transformation\n\n![](images/d1/d175f9eba3a6da7cc7750b3e59fe0e5904df27511a6b64481598e0f0ebfa1f0c.svg) \n\nis a $C^1$ transformation, meaning that it and its first derivatives are continuous, then the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFor example, to compute the Jacobian determinant of our transformation above, we first calculate the required partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡v & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v|\\begin{aligned}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\end{aligned}|+u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w & -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡w & u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot {u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡w+{sin}^{2}⁡w)+u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot u{sin}^{2}⁡v\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡w+{sin}^{2}⁡w) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v+{u}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡v+{sin}^{2}⁡v) \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's get some practice at computing Jacobians of three-dimensional transformations." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130463 #edb/ref 1065 7237 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130463 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "610a6348-ed80-507a-b390-560e9bc1e4ef" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130463 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130469 #edb/ref 1064 "The **critical points** of a transformation $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^3\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^3,$ given by\n\n![](images/0f/0f47dfd79a5147b5628975b26ea21a37288d809fb30a5a90b205ecc7b25ae008.svg)\n\nare the points in Cartesian $uvw$-space where the Jacobian is zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}=0\n$$\n\n  \n\nSimilar to the two-dimensional case, the transformation $\\mathbf T$ is locally non-invertible at the critical points.\n\nFinally, provided that $\\mathbf{T}$ is invertible, the connection between the Jacobians of $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right)^{-1}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130469 #edb/ref 1065 7676 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130469 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9dd5b9bc-94c3-5fd0-8752-3fa9be521934" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130469 #edb/ref 1067 "The Critical Points of a Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130473 #edb/ref 1064 "The Jacobian determinant of a spatial transformation can be interpreted similarly to the Jacobian of a plane transformation.\n\nAs before, the Jacobian describes *local* effects, not global ones. \n\nGiven that $J(u_0,v_0,w_0)$ denotes the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}$ evaluated at $(u_0, v_0, w_0),$ let's describe $\\mathbf T$'s effect on a *tiny* rectangular prism in Cartesian $uvw$-space whose bottom, back left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0,w_0).$\n\n![](images/31/31c0a7275acdac0dc217e73f3c1e3d9efe504832419c87486cee980e20ea04da.png)\n\n \n\nIt can be shown that, provided that our prism is very small and $(u_0,v_0, w_0)$ is not a critical point of $\\mathbf T,$ the image of our prism can be approximated by a parallelepiped. \n\nThe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ evaluated at $(u_0,v_0,w_0)$ describes the following key features between our tiny rectangular prism and its (approximate) image:\n\n- The local *volume* scale factor of $\\mathbf T$. \n\nSuppose the length, width, and height of our rectangular prism are $\\delta u,$ $\\delta v,$ and $\\delta w,$ so its volume is \n\n$$\n\\delta V = \\delta u \\delta v \\delta w.\n$$\n\n Then, it can be shown that the approximate parallelepiped in $xyz$-space has a volume equal to \n\n$$\n\\left|J(u_0,v_0,w_0)\\right| \\cdot \\delta V,\n$$\n\n as depicted below.\n\n![](images/d6/d669cd5369d33e618ab43cfd3e93ee70c9fea8420b5a829d78826c11524511aa.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* orientation-preserving or *locally* orientation-reversing.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0,w_0) > 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving. \n\nGeometrically, this means that the transformation maps a triple of vectors onto a triple with the same orientation (right-handed $\\to$ right-handed or left-handed $\\to$ left-handed).\n\n![](images/f0/f05f9a79d9156518e948c46f6c67eb5ff31132fd4a088e14219eada7eb49a441.png)\n\n \n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0, w_0) < 0,$ the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the transformation maps a triple of vectors onto a triple with the opposite orientation (right-handed $\\to$ left-handed or left-handed $\\to$ right-handed).\n\n![](images/45/45b08e0fec7114db52a05cb42ed46d7d62baa5b01a5dea37752928348802b205.png)\n\n- Whether the transformation is *locally* invertible or *locally* non-invertible.\n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0,w_0) \\neq 0,$ the transformation is (locally) invertible.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the transformation maps our tiny rectangular prism (a three-dimensional region) onto a three-dimensional region (i.e., a parallelepiped).\n\n![](images/61/61557eb2d78d468946375b918ab182da518b7cf2c68a1c0a0593c93b991bcf78.png)\n\n \n\nIf $J(u_0,v_0,w_0)= 0,$ the transformation is (locally) non-invertible.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the transformation maps our tiny rectangular prism (a three-dimensional region) onto a two-dimensional region (a parallelogram), a one-dimensional region (a line segment), or a zero-dimensional region (a point).\n\n![](images/72/726ba518de982ddd0b2924a645463b496ed881ead76a652c812c7a164aa684b8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130473 #edb/ref 1065 7718 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130473 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "10a3ad60-7124-5508-a053-b827183f5a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186130473 #edb/ref 1067 "Intuition Behind the Jacobian" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278815 #edb/ref 1081 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278815 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186278815 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "18d889e7-c824-5206-ae1f-be2ca84ac201" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279228 #edb/ref 1081 "6{u}^{3}v-{v}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186279228 #e